// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "--help" 1`] = `
"OpenCove CLI — Control projects, spaces, canvas nodes and worker runtimes

Usage: opencove <command> [options]

Workspace:
  project  List projects and find their IDs
  space    List and inspect project spaces
  node     Create, read, update and delete canvas nodes
  task     Claim and release tasks for existing agents
  note     Append text to shared notes
  canvas   Focus nodes and spaces on the canvas
  agent    List, inspect, prompt, wait for and read agents
  events   Stream agent observations and resume from a saved cursor

Introspection:
  skill    Print the bundled agent usage guide
  whoami   Show your caller identity and permission scope
  context  Orient yourself in a project and space
  api      Discover operations, inspect input schemas and invoke the Control Surface

Runtime:
  ping      Check whether the Control Surface responds
  status    Show protocol, app version and instance identity
  worker    Start, inspect and stop local workers
  runtime   Inspect, verify and prepare managed runtimes
  endpoint  Register and manage remote worker connections
  mount     Connect project folders to worker endpoints
  fs        Read, write and inspect files through the Control Surface
  worktree  List, create and archive Git worktrees
  session   Run agents and inspect or stop their sessions
  pty       Start and attach to terminal sessions

Plugins:
  plugin  Discover, install and manage plugins

Selectors:
  Node, space and project options accept @self, an exact ID, an exact name (case-sensitive), or a unique ID prefix of 6+ characters.
  @self uses your terminal identity. Names are scoped by --project or your own project inside OpenCove; explicit --space constrains every node selector, including full IDs. Ambiguous targets require a full ID.

Global options:
  --endpoint <url>         Connect to this Control Surface URL
  --token <token>          Authenticate with this bearer token
  --user-data <dir>        Use this profile directory
  --timeout <ms>           Set the request timeout in milliseconds (default: 20000)
  --pretty                 Indent JSON output for readability
  --limit <n>              Limit output lists to n items (0 means all); cut object lists add truncated totals, bare arrays write one stderr notice
  --fields <a,b,...>       Select item-relative fields; preserve envelopes and accept item-key prefixes. api call queries also accept raw output paths
  -o, --output json|table  Choose JSON (default; agent read/session final default to message text) or a supported table layout
  -h, --help               Show help for this command or group
  --version                Print the CLI version

Exit codes: 0 success; 1 operation failure; 2 invalid input; 3 unreachable; 4 unauthorized; 5 incompatible protocol; 6 ambiguous target; 10 waiting observed; 11 agent exited during wait; 124 timeout; 130 interrupted.

Run "opencove <group> --help" for commands in a group.
Start with "opencove api ops --domains" for a compact domain map.
Then run "opencove api ops --prefix node --fields id,cliCommand" to explore one domain.
Run "opencove api ops" only when you need the full catalog.
AI agents: run \`opencove skill\` for a usage guide.
"
`;

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "node" 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove node <command> [options]

Commands:
  list                                       List canvas nodes
  get                                        Get a canvas node
  create <note|task|website|agent|terminal>  Create canvas nodes
  update <note|task|website|agent|terminal>  Update canvas nodes
  delete                                     Delete a canvas node

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "node" 2`] = `
"Usage: opencove node create note [--near <selector> | --root-canvas | --space <id> | <other-target-space-options>] [options]

Create a note node

For agents creating helper nodes, recommend --near @self to place them near the caller in its current Space (or root). --near is opt-in; combine with --root-canvas to place outside Spaces. Size-only --frame (width/height) is allowed with --near; x/y are refused.

Without explicit x/y, root-canvas placement searches next to you when run inside OpenCove and avoids Spaces. If your node belongs to another project, the search starts at the canvas origin. Use --focus to reveal the node. A --frame with both x and y places the node exactly there, even if it overlaps a Space.

Default target inside OpenCove: your current Space, or the root canvas when you are outside any Space. An explicit --project alone does not select a container; creation also needs --near, --root-canvas or a Space target.

Target container (choose one):
  --root-canvas                                 Create on the project's root canvas, outside every Space
  --space <id>                                  Select a space by its ID; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --space-name <name> [--project <id>]          Find a named space, optionally within a project
  --worker <id-or-name> --branch <branch>       Find a space by worker and Git branch
  --worker <id-or-name> --path <absolute-path>  Find a space by worker and working directory
  --project <id>                                Select the root-canvas project, or narrow a Space lookup; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Options:
  --text <text>         Set the note contents
  --text-file <path|->  Read text from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin
  --title <text>        Set the node title
  --frame <json>        Set x, y, width and height as a JSON object. Sizes outside a kind's range are adjusted to fit; note: width 220–360, height 140–260; inspect appliedFrame and frameAdjusted for the actual applied size
  --near <selector>     Place near a reference node; recommend --near @self for helper nodes; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --focus               Focus after creation
  -q, --quiet           Print only the created object identifier followed by a newline

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, spawnedBy, lineageDepth, lineageStatus, data, data.kind, data.text

Field descriptions:
  spawnedBy      ID of the node that created this node; null for a root or unavailable lineage
  lineageDepth   A node started by the user has lineageDepth 1; each agent-created generation adds one. Null when lineage is unavailable
  lineageStatus  recorded when creation is recorded, root when no parent is recorded, unavailable when lineage cannot be read

Projection example:
  opencove node create note --space space-123 --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"projectId":"p","spaceId":"space-123","node":{"id":"n","title":"Note"}}

Examples:
  opencove node create note --space space-123 --title "Release checklist" --text "Run the smoke tests"
  opencove node create note --root-canvas --project project-123 --text "Canvas note" --focus
  opencove node create note --space-name "Sprint planning" --project project-123 --text-file ./notes.md --focus
  printf 'Review the release notes\\n' | opencove node create note --space space-123 --text-file -

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "note" 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove note append --node <selector> --text <text> [options]

Append text to a shared note

Provide exactly one of --text or --text-file; use --text-file - for stdin. Preserves whitespace and separates entries with a blank line. Each append is limited to 64 KiB of UTF-8 text; the resulting note must fit within 1 MiB. Concurrent appends preserve commit order. Repeating a request may duplicate text; inspect the note after an uncertain outcome.

Options:
  --node <selector>     Select a note to append to; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --text <text>         Append literal text without trimming whitespace (required)
  --text-file <path|->  Read text from a UTF-8 file, or use - for stdin
  --space <selector>    Require every node selector, including full IDs, to belong to this space; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)
  --project <id>        Narrow target lookup to a project; also accepts @self, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: revision, nodeId, appendedBytes, totalBytes

Projection example:
  opencove note append --node '<selector>' --text '<text>' --fields revision
  {"revision":2}

Examples:
  opencove note append --node "Progress" --text "Review complete"
  opencove note append --node "Progress" --text-file ./result.txt
  opencove note append --node "Progress" --text-file -

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "task" 1`] = `
"Usage: opencove task claim --task <selector> [options]

Claim a task for an existing agent

Defaults to your authenticated agent. With a user credential, supply --agent. You can select your own agent or any agent you started, including their descendants. Task and selected agent must share a project and Space. An existing holder must release the task before another agent can claim it. Claiming does not start an agent.

Options:
  --task <selector>   Select the task to claim; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --agent <selector>  Select an existing agent; session grants can select their own agent or agents they started; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters)

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, data, data.kind, data.requirement, data.status, data.priority, data.tags, data.linkedAgentNodeId, revision, changed

Projection example:
  opencove task claim --task '<selector>' --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"node":{"id":"n","title":"Task"}}

Examples:
  opencove task claim --task "Review"
  opencove task claim --task task-123 --agent "Reviewer" --pretty

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

exports[`TypeScript CLI core > help snapshot "task" 2`] = `
"Usage: opencove task release --task <selector> [options]

Release a task without stopping its agent

The current holder, a coordinator that started the holder, or an authenticated user can release a task. Defaults to todo; use ai_done when the work is ready for review, or done when finished. Release preserves task history and does not stop the agent.

Options:
  --task <selector>             Select the task to release; also accepts @self, @parent, an exact name or a unique ID prefix (6+ characters) (required)
  --status <todo|ai_done|done>  Set the task status after release; defaults to todo

Table output (-o table): not supported.
Projectable fields: id, kind, title, projectId, spaceId, frame, frame.x, frame.y, frame.width, frame.height, status, sessionId, data, data.kind, data.requirement, data.status, data.priority, data.tags, data.linkedAgentNodeId, revision, changed

Projection example:
  opencove task release --task '<selector>' --fields id,title
  {"revision":1,"node":{"id":"n","title":"Task"}}

Examples:
  opencove task release --task "Review" --status ai_done

Global options: see "opencove --help".
"
`;

